# Build output
/bin/
/dist/
# The release notes the workflow (and a local changelog-notes dry run) renders
# at the repo root — a throwaway artifact, never source.
/notes.md
*.exe

# Go test / coverage artifacts
*.test
*.out
coverage.*

# Python bytecode. The deploy/ script tests run as `python3 <file>`, which caches
# nothing for __main__ — but importing or running one any other way does, and a
# committed .pyc is stale the moment its source changes.
__pycache__/

# Eval run artifacts (report.json, summary.md, failed-trial transcripts). A run
# is a point-in-time record of one endpoint's behaviour, not source.
/evals/artifacts/

# Go workspace files (local-only)
go.work
go.work.sum

# Local configuration and secrets — never commit these
.env
.env.*
*.local.yaml
*.local.yml
# ...but keep the committed templates, and ignore any real model-providers.json
# (a filled-in copy carries a live api_key) wherever it is created.
!.env.example
model-providers.json

# Tooling caches
.impeccable/

# Editors / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# Claude Code personal settings (the shared .claude/settings.json IS committed)
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
# Parallel-session worktrees live here; without this every worktree's checkout
# shows up as untracked files in the main one. See .worktreeinclude.
.claude/worktrees/

# Build outputs at the repo root. `go build ./cmd/<name>` and `-o <name>` both
# drop their binary here, and a `git add -A` then sweeps it in. Every cmd/ and
# tools/ name is listed rather than just the one that bit us: /gate alone was
# covered when #333 committed an 80 MB worker (#338), so a partial list is how
# this recurs. Anchored to the root — cmd/brain/ and friends are directories
# that must stay tracked.
/brain
/changelog
/controlplane
/executor
/gate
/worker
# A literal `%SystemDrive%` directory: a reviewer's sandboxed Windows shell left
# the variable unexpanded and wrote its icon cache under it at the repo root,
# and the same `git add -A` sweep then committed ~1 MB of .db files (plan 36
# slice 3's review round). Anchored to the root like the binaries above.
/%SystemDrive%/

# Terraform working directories, state and variable files. The provider plugins
# and the tfvars are machine-local, and so is `foundation/`'s state; since #478
# `environment/`'s authoritative state is a GCS bucket, and a terraform.tfstate
# beside its .tf files is a pre-migration leftover — still ignored, and still
# not something to commit. .terraform.lock.hcl is NOT ignored — it pins provider
# versions and belongs in the repo, exactly like go.sum.
#
# Matched ANYWHERE in the tree, not only under deploy/gcp/*/. A path-scoped rule
# is a trap here, because what it fails to match is a file that routinely holds
# credentials in plaintext: a `terraform` command run from the repository root —
# by a person, a reviewer, or a tool — writes ./terraform.tfstate, which the old
# rule did not cover, and one was staged for commit before this was widened. An
# empty state is harmless; the next one would not have been.
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
# `*.tfvars.*` rather than just `*.tfvars.json`, for the reason `*.tfstate.*`
# above is here and one more of its own: tfvars.sh writes through
# `mktemp "$OUT.XXXXXX"` beside the destination, so that the final `mv` is atomic
# within one directory. Its EXIT trap removes that file on every exit it can see,
# but not on SIGKILL or a power loss — and what survives holds the project id and
# the Cloud Build service-account email. Untracked but NOT ignored is precisely
# the state in which `git add -A` sweeps a file into a public repository (#356).
*.tfvars.*
tfplan
**/tfplan
# A generated Helm values file, in case one is written here anyway — the README
# says to keep it outside the tree precisely because credentials get appended to it.
deploy/gcp/**/values*.yaml
